, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ron_Herron\">Ron Herron</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Dennis_Crompton&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">Dennis Crompton</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Webb_(architect)\">Michael Webb</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=David_Greene_(architect)&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">David Greene</a>.","subtitle":"Peter Cook, Warren Chalk, Ron Herron, Dennis Crompton, Michael Webb and David Greene","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/archigram.jpg","external":"http://designmuseum.org/design/archigram","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/36+Bedford+Square,+AA+School+of+Architecture,+Fitzrovia,+London+WC1B+3ES,+UK/@51.5183066,-0.1307022,17z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":770.36,"topicId":"scene","topicLabel":"Scene"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":344.88,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1125.6,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1192.04,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":644.08,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1356.68,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"}]},"British Airports Authority Office, Heathrow":{"slug":"british-airports-authority-office-heathrow","body":"Here on a site adjacent to one of the main runways at London Airport, the strict height controls and massive noise interference anabled one to utilise the unusable – in the form of a narrow, road-boudned strip of land. Roof-parking, helping the sound insulation, enabled multiple spot access to the very centre of this single-floor, open-plan office – the ideal access condiction for burolandschaft.  (CP in The Square Book, p. 43)","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":669.44,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"BTDB Computer Centre":{"slug":"btdb-computer-centre","body":"A lightweight building simply sitting on a reinforced concrete raft. The steel columns had to be sufficiently precise to take window gaskets.  (Frank Newby in The Square Book, p. 82)","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":677.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1763.6,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"}]},"Buckminster Fuller":{"slug":"buckminster-fuller","body":"Richard Buckminster \"Bucky\" Fuller published more than 30 books, coining or popularizing terms such as \"<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceship_Earth\">Spaceship Earth</a>\", <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephemeralization\">ephemeralization</a>, and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synergetics_(Fuller)\">synergetic</a>. He also developed numerous inventions, mainly architectural designs, and popularized the widely known <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geodesic_dome\">geodesic dome</a>. Carbon molecules known as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fullerene\">fullerenes</a> were later named by scientists for their resemblance to geodesic spheres. Buckminster Fuller was the second president of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mensa_International\">Mensa</a> from 1974 to 1983.","subtitle":"Neo-futuristic architect, systems theorist, author, designer, and inventor.","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/buckminster-fuller.jpg","external":"https://bfi.org","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Milton,+MA,+USA/@42.2495321,-71.0661653,7z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":438.2,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1350.68,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":123.44,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":549.32,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1393.16,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"Cambridge University":{"slug":"cambridge-university","body":"The Department of Architecture is part of the Faculty of Architecture and History of Art in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Cambridge\">University of Cambridge</a>. Both Departments are housed in Scroope Terrace on Trumpington Street and they share a library. The Faculty celebrated its Centenary in 2012. The <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Cambridge\">University of Cambridge</a> is one of the world's leading Universities and the Architecture Department is consistently rated in the top three in national surveys.\r\n\r\nThe school has a strong research reputation in both history/theory and in scientific research, particularly into modeling and sustainable design.","subtitle":"Faculty of Architecture","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/cambridge-university.jpg","external":"http://www.arct.cam.ac.uk","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Faculty+Of+Architecture+%26+History+Of+Art/@52.1984,0.121524,17z","also":[{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":475.92,"topicId":null,"topicLabel":null},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1188.32,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":216.68,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":696.36,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"}]},"Cedric Price Office":{"slug":"cedric-price-office","body":"","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":444.04,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1751.88,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":43.4,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":1.64,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":314.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":442.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":184.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":247.32,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1065.2,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1063.84,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":272.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":181.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":466.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":26.88,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":716.28,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":743.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1639.24,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":2.64,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":568.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":672,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":761.16,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"Claude Parent & Paul Virilio":{"slug":"claude-parent-paul-virilio-2","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Claude Parent, born on 23 February 1923 at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuilly-sur-Seine\">Neuilly-sur-Seine</a>, France, is a French <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a> known for his buildings featuring sloped floors, an aesthetic initially inspired by Parent's discovery, with philosopher <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Virilio\">Paul Virilio</a>, of World War II bunkers constructed along the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall\">Atlantic Wall</a> that had accidentally slipped down sand dunes because of severe winter weather. \"Inside [the fallen bunkers], you tumbled through a strange room; the floor was so sloped that you couldn't tell whether what you were standing on was a slanted floor or a wall,\" Parent has said. With Virilio, who was also a teacher at the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89cole_sp%C3%A9ciale_d%27architecture\">École spéciale d’architecture</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris\">Paris</a>, they educated many contemporary architects, such as Jean Nouvel. He won the Grand Prix national de l'architecture in 1979 and was elected at the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acad%C3%A9mie_des_Beaux-Arts\">Académie des Beaux-Arts</a> in 2005.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":341.04,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1224.48,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"FUN PALACE":{"slug":"fun-palace","body":"Fun Palace is Cedric Price’s most celebrated work. Whether characterised as a giant toy or as a building-sized transformable machine, the project’s interest resides in its radical reliance on structure and technology, its exemplification of notions of time-based and anticipatory architecture. With Fun Palace, Price addressed social and political issues that go far beyond the typical bounds of architecture. Fun Palace was conceived and commissioned in 1961 by renowned theatre director and producer Joan Littlewood. Price developed plans for the project through 1964, both for the main project and for a smaller, more mobile “pilot” project. Neither were realised. Attempts to get planning permission for a wide variety of sites within and around London continued through 1970, amidst opposition from church, citizen groups and confounded city councils.","subtitle":"Laboratory of Fun and a University of the Streets","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/fun-palace.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/283-cedric-price-fun-palace","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.518722,-0.0007261,13z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":139.88,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":464.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":647.24,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":11,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":381,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":671.76,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1034.84,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1547.08,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":414.28,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1021.72,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1036.76,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1132.72,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":873.92,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1684.84,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":744.8,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":796.08,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":883.28,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":676.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1117.36,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1903.76,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":651.64,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":83.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":138.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":637.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":681.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1943.6,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1104.36,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"}]},"GLC":{"slug":"glc","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The Greater London Council (GLC) was the top-tier <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_government\">local government</a> administrative body for <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_London\">Greater London</a> from 1965 to 1986. It replaced the earlier <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_County_Council\">London County Council</a> (LCC) which had covered a much smaller area. The GLC was dissolved in 1986 by the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_Government_Act_1985\">Local Government Act 1985</a> and its powers were devolved to the London boroughs and other entities.</p>","subtitle":"Greater London Council","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/glc.jpg","external":"http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/march/31/newsid_2530000/2530803.stm","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":205.2,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":492.2,"topicId":"southbank","topicLabel":"SOUTHBANK"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":143,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1549.2,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"International Dialogue of Experimental Architecture ":{"slug":"international-dialogue-of-experimental-architecture","body":"The International Dialogue of Experimental Architecture (IDEA) conference at Folkestone in 1966 featured a roster of speakers including Cedric Price, Buckminster Fuller, Reyner Banham, Hans Hollein, Claude Parent and Frei Otto.","subtitle":"Folkestone","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/international-dialogue-of-experimental-architecture.jpg","external":"http://radical-pedagogies.com/search-cases/e17-international-dialogue-experimental-architecture-idea/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Folkestone,+Kent,+UK","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1175.4,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"New Society":{"slug":"new-society","body":"New Society was a weekly magazine of social inquiry and social and cultural comment, published in the United Kingdom from 1962 to 1988. It drew on the disciplines of sociology, anthropology, psychology, human geography, social history and social policy, and it published wide-ranging social reportage. The magazine was launched by a small, London-based independent publishing house, Harrison Raison, which in 1956 had successfully launched <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Scientist\">New Scientist</a>, a weekly magazine to serve the natural sciences. The idea was to create a comparable magazine about the social sciences. The cultural commentator <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Hewison\">Robert Hewison</a> wrote that New Society became \"a forum for the new <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligentsia\">intelligentsia</a>\", crested by the expansion of higher education in Britain from the early 1960s. New Society was usually perceived as centre-left, but it was fiercely non-partisan and never endorsed any political party. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Raison\">Timothy Raison</a> (1962–68), its founding editor, was later a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Party_(UK)\">Conservative</a> MP from 1970 until 1992. In Raison's opinion, New Society sought \"to mirror, to analyse, to understand, not to exhort or moralise.\" It tried to see the world as it was, not as it was supposed to be. These aims were continued and developed under the editorship of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Barker_(writer)\">Paul Barker</a> (1968–86), who was described by the labour historian <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Hobsbawm\">Eric Hobsbawm</a> as the \"most original of editors\".","subtitle":"Magazine","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/new-society.jpg","external":"http://www.timeshighereducation.co.uk/162372.article","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1329.44,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2116.64,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":29.6,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":146.16,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1181.08,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]},"Open University":{"slug":"open-university","body":"The Open University (OU) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distance_education\">distance learning</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Research\">research</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University\">university</a> founded by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Charter\">Royal Charter</a> in the United Kingdom. The university is funded by a combination of student fees, contract income, and allocations for teaching and research by the higher education funding bodies throughout the UK. It is notable for having an open entry policy, i.e. students' previous academic achievements are not taken into account for entry to most <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undergraduate_education\">undergraduate</a> courses.","subtitle":"Distance Learning and Research University","image":null,"external":"http://www.open.ac.uk","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=open+university","also":[{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":771.72,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":566.76,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"Paul Barker":{"slug":"paul-barker","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Barker was educated at local schools in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calder_Valley\">Calder Valley</a> and won an <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exhibition_(scholarship)\">Exhibition (scholarship)</a> to <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brasenose_College,_Oxford\">Brasenose College, Oxford</a>, to read French. Before taking up his place at Oxford, he did <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_service\">National service</a> and was commissioned as an officer in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligence_Corps_(United_Kingdom)\">Intelligence Corps</a>, and while in the Army studied <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_language\">Russian language</a> at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge_University\">Cambridge University</a>. After taking his Oxford degree, he then went on to the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89cole_Normale_Sup%C3%A9rieure\">École Normale Supérieure</a> in Paris for a year as lecteur. He joined the London staff of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Times\">The Times</a> in 1959, but early in 1964 left to join the recently founded <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Society\">New Society</a> as a staff writer. He went on to <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Economist\">The Economist</a>, but returned to New Society almost at once - in 1965 - as deputy editor. In 1968 he succeeded <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Raison\">Timothy Raison</a>, the first editor of New Society, and edited the magazine until 1986. Subsequently, he was a columnist on <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sunday_Times\">The Sunday Times</a> and a regular writer for the London <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evening_Standard\">Evening Standard</a>, the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Times_Literary_Supplement\">Times Literary Supplement</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prospect_Magazine\">Prospect Magazine</a>. He is currently a senior research fellow with the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.youngfoundation.org.uk/\">Young Foundation</a>, as well as being a freelance journalist, broadcaster and author.</p>","subtitle":"Journalist and writer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/paul-barker.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Barker_(writer)","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1331.12,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2117.92,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1183.68,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]},"Peter Cook":{"slug":"peter-cook","body":"Professor Sir Peter Cook RA, founder of Archigram, former Director the Institute for Contemporary Art, London (the ICA) and Bartlett School of Architecture at University Col- lege, London has been a pivotal figure within the global architectural world for over half a century. His ongoing contribution to architectural innovation was most recent- ly recognised via the conferral of an honorary doctorate in April 2010, by the University of Lund, Sweden.","subtitle":"Archigram","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/peter-cook.jpg","external":"http://www.crab-studio.com/assets/cv-p.cook%2c-g.-robotham.pdf","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=Crab+Studio","also":[{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":1080.88,"topicId":"delight","topicLabel":"Delight"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1197.4,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1355.16,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"}]},"Peter Reyner Banham":{"slug":"peter-reyner-banham","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Peter Reyner Banham, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FRIBA\">FRIBA</a> (2 March 1922 – 19 March 1988) was an English architectural critic and prolific writer best known for his theoretical treatise Theory and Design in the First Machine Age (1960) and for his 1971 book Los Angeles: The Architecture of Four Ecologies.<sup>[1]</sup> In the latter he categorized the Los Angeles experience into four ecological models (Surfurbia, Foothills, The Plains of Id, and Autopia) and explored the distinct architectural cultures of each ecology. Banham worked in London, but lived primarily in the United States from the late 1960s until the end of his life.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1340.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":406.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":57.84,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":824.96,"topicId":"non-plan","topicLabel":"NON PLAN"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1075.8,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1178.8,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]},"Pop-up Parliament":{"slug":"pop-up-parliament","body":"It was announced this week that the statues of former prime ministers that stand in the House of Commons, the lower house of the British parliament, are to be protected from members of Parliament who touch them for luck. According to Melanie Unwin, deputy curator of the Houses of Parliament, “there are now cracks and small holes on the surface of the [Winston] Churchill statue and substantial loss of surface texture on other statues.” In 1965, Price proposed a Pop-up Parliament to replace the existing one. \"If we want an efficient parliament, let’s give it a whole efficient building to work . . . replace the present historic monument with an up-to-date structure – flexible, accessible and dispensable,” he wrote.","subtitle":"Cedric Price","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/pop-up-parliament.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/2166-a-pop-up-parliament","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":496,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1589.88,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1896.04,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2111.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":377.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"Potteries Thinkbelt housing types":{"slug":"potteries-thinkbelt-housing-types","body":"Four types of housing were designed: crate, battery, capsule and sprawl. All are designed by name to goad a profession hard-wired into rejecting such terms having any relation to the sacrosanct Vitruvian principles of ‘commodity, firmness and delight’.","subtitle":"Crate, battery, capsule and sprawl","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/potteries-thinkbelt-housing-types.jpg","external":"http://www.amazon.com/Cedric-Price-Potteries-Thinkbelt-SuperCrit/dp/0415434122","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Madeley,+Staffordshire,+UK/@52.997861,-2.339182,7z","also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":232.04,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":612.24,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1319.68,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"Potteries Thinkbelt":{"slug":"potteries-thinkbelt","body":"Price adopted the advancement of science and engineering working with industry as core content for his scheme – the integration of manufacturing and learning – but, took the challenge of changing attitudes towards education a step further. The opening statement of the publication of his project in New Society opens with: “Higher education and further education should become a major industrial undertaking, not a service run by gentlemen for the few.” His ‘Thinkbelt’ – the neologism alone asks that we free up preconceptions of the typology - goes beyond both the traditional monastic form of “polite cathedral town amenity” and the then current trend for building huge new campus’s on isolated greenfield sites.","subtitle":"A city caused by learning","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/potteries-thinkbelt.jpg","external":"https://vimeo.com/37940157\nhttp://www.supercrits.com/1/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Keele+University/@53.0084306,-2.2615387,14z","also":[{"slug":"be